.SH "BUGS"
The \fBtable_parameter\fR regex will raise an \fBAttributeError\fR rather than skipping gracefully if any argument in \fBsys\.argv\fR does not match the \fB\-\-table=\fR pattern, because \fBre\.match\fR returns \fBNone\fR for non\-matching strings and \fBNone\.groups()\fR is called unconditionally\.
.P
The error message for a missing \fB\-\-table\fR argument reads \fB"No \-\-class argument supplied"\fR rather than \fB"No \-\-table argument supplied"\fR, which is misleading\.
.P
The \fBtarget\fR variable is assigned inside the argument\-parsing loop but is referenced unconditionally in the return statement\. If no bare column argument is supplied, \fBtarget\fR will be undefined and the script will raise a \fBNameError\fR rather than a descriptive \fBIllegalArgumentError\fR\.
.P
The bare \fBexcept: raise\fR in \fB__main__\fR discards exception context and offers no additional diagnostic information beyond what the original exception already provides\.
.P
The broad \fBexcept: raise IllegalArgumentError(\|\.\|\.\|\.)\fR in the relational column\-validation branch will mask any underlying database error (such as a connection failure or a malformed query) with the generic message \fB"Unexpected error for querying column"\fR, making diagnosis difficult\.
.P
\fBsqlite3\fR is imported unconditionally at the top of the script regardless of the value of \fBRECORD_DBTYPE\fR, which will cause an import error on systems where the \fBsqlite3\fR module is unavailable even when the backend is configured as \fBpostgres\fR or \fBgremlin\fR\.
